Z||000012||fierojunky||04-03-2001||08:52 AM||||What engine S-10?  Was it the old 2.8 cuz the new S-10's with the 4.3 come in two forms 180HP and 190HP (in the ZR2's and the Extremes) so 210 is easily achieved  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000013||1FST2M6||04-03-2001||09:10 AM||||Ed has been CRAZY busy.. you know the shop was bought by <A HREF="http://www.enthuzacar.com" TARGET=_blank>www.enthuzacar.com</A>  and Chad has made a LOT of SWEET changes. but in those changes time off isn't one of them..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <P>I've got shaved heads which nearly made me fail my emissions test.. and i'm pretty sure i'll fail next year... this year the emissions guys just pulled the sniffer out and laid it near my exhaut.. my NOx was WAY too high.. (high combustion temps) the SE would do the same thing... oh well, have you tried to call the guys at design one? ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Predator||04-03-2001||10:26 AM||||I emailed Kevin at Design One, and he replied he would look at the Dyno run closer when he had time, and that was the last I heard from him. That's the first I have heard of Ed's shop being bought. I hope they will still work on custom mods. Let me know when you can get together. I am not sure Kevin can really diagnose the lean condition with just the dyno run to go by, but I will call him to get a status. I can't afford to just start changing out parts hoping to hit the right thing. Also, on the topic of how much power the stock computer can handle. When I bought my turbo it came in a Stage I and Stage II. The Stage I uses the stock ECM and produces around 200. The Stage II is supposed to produce 225. It has to have a modified ECM so I would guess that anything over 200 and you will need to get the ECM tweeked. Just my .02 worth.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||ThatRickGuy||04-03-2001||10:31 AM||||I could see 220 at the flywheel. Of course it would help if you could include stroking the engine (3.1 crank), bore it out (3.2) shave the heads, 1.6 rockers, stepped up cam, headers, bored intake, gasket match everything, a good job on the heads and valves, beef up the injectors and fuel pump... as long as you don't get the cam too wild, and you use a sniffer to balance out the fuel/air mixture. The computer should be able to handle it as long as you can adjust the fuel preasure to match the new intake. Altho, with a new comp you could switch cams and get way more power.||||reg||1
Z||000016||1FST2M6||04-03-2001||11:20 AM||||OH yeah their still doing custom stuff. just not on Civics any more.. Miatas, Acuras, and Focus are the new drug of choice. and your and my fiero.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> they've already built a low 13second nitrous Focus and are installing a S/C on a one today. the lean condtion is the computers fault.. we've got a couple people running Split second fuel tuning boxes and their fuel curve looks JUST like yours. i know you don't have that systems but it gave ed a general idea where to start changing things. uuum...  keep us posted on Kevins ideas. and the computer was modified to understand and respond to the knock sensor in your car Predator. if you put a knock sensor on a high performance N/A car it'll run like CRAP.. the pistons tend to slap the cyl walls and the rattle fom the increased compression will just send the senor to the nut house. killing any timing you might have.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Will||04-03-2001||02:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by olliver:<BR><B>I am just about to under go a HUGE engine rebuild (2.8L).  The guy who's doing it wants to know what the max h.p. that the computer can handle?...will it go into shut down?...<BR>If anyone has ANY info this would be great.<P>I was guaranteed 220 h.p. but i don't want the computer to crash.<P>Thanx <BR>Olliver</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I've heard rumors of 2.8's putting out 270 HP, but those engines are WAY too radical for the street.<P>I'd guess that the engine would have to run to 7000 RPM to get 220 HP on pump gas and street compression. You're builder <I>is</I> building it to take pump gas, right?<P>If you found the right size of injectors (significantly larger than stock) and fiddled with the fuel pressure you might be able to stretch the stock program to work. Maybe.<P>I'd say to get it to run really well, you'll be looking at a custom program.<BR>Go to <A HREF="http://www.diy-efi.org" TARGET=_blank>www.diy-efi.org</A>  to learn how to program your own ECM.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||JR||04-03-2001||09:04 PM||||II doubt that the computer can handle more tthan what PISA offers in their 2.8 upgrade rrebuild kit (160-190hp).  Their kit was tthorougly engineered including porting and ppolishing to give a reliable 190hp.<BR>HHowever I can assure all you doubting TThomas's, that 270hp!!! is easily aattainable.  I have had it done, just ffollow GM's Handbook of performance engine bbuilding.<BR>Mmy engine in the 88GT was tore down then bbored 30over, built up with a polished and ttreated balanced and knifeedeged 3.1 crank aalso grooved oiling holes for improved llubrication with a 90psi heavy duty oil pump.<BR>IInstalled Wiesco forged pistons 11:1 on chevy small block shot peened connecting rods.<P>Heads (stock 2.8)were ported (careful, not much metal here)and polished.  Valves are get this now, titanium 3angle with titanium retainers with small block double springs.  Crane narrow roller rockers on short block 7/16 rocker posts.  Valvetrain is operated by a high lift crower mechanical cam.<P>The most difficult part was finding an intake manifold that would allow for deep breathing, and nothing from GM available.  So a call to offenhauser elicited a specially cast intake manifold(1500$) to accomodated the Holley 650 double pumper.  This is the trick to getting 250-270hp on pump gas, you see it must breath then it must be able to rev high 7500rpm.  Use titanium and forged pistons and polished crank with improved oiling to make it last.<P>------------------<BR>85GT (daily driver 120,000 miles)<BR>88GT (highly modified sunday driver)||||reg||1
Z||000019||Will||04-04-2001||11:10 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JR:<BR><B>II doubt that the computer can handle more tthan what PISA offers in their 2.8 upgrade rrebuild kit (160-190hp).  Their kit was tthorougly engineered including porting and ppolishing to give a reliable 190hp.<BR>HHowever I can assure all you doubting TThomas's, that 270hp!!! is easily aattainable.  I have had it done, just ffollow GM's Handbook of performance engine bbuilding.<BR>Mmy engine in the 88GT was tore down then bbored 30over, built up with a polished and ttreated balanced and knifeedeged 3.1 crank aalso grooved oiling holes for improved llubrication with a 90psi heavy duty oil pump.<BR>IInstalled Wiesco forged pistons 11:1 on chevy small block shot peened connecting rods.<P>Heads (stock 2.8)were ported (careful, not much metal here)and polished.  Valves are get this now, titanium 3angle with titanium retainers with small block double springs.  Crane narrow roller rockers on short block 7/16 rocker posts.  Valvetrain is operated by a high lift crower mechanical cam.<P>The most difficult part was finding an intake manifold that would allow for deep breathing, and nothing from GM available.  So a call to offenhauser elicited a specially cast intake manifold(1500$) to accomodated the Holley 650 double pumper.  This is the trick to getting 250-270hp on pump gas, you see it must breath then it must be able to rev high 7500rpm.  Use titanium and forged pistons and polished crank with improved oiling to make it last.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I bet that wasn't cheap!<BR>How does that beast stack up against LS1 F-bodies and Corvettes?<BR>Are you using a Getrag?<P>It's not a 2.8, it's a 3.2. (Bored 0.030 over with 3.1 crank).||||reg||1
Z||000020||GT Bastard||04-04-2001||12:34 PM||||JR, you need to get that dyno'd.  Got any pics of the build-up?||||reg||1
Z||000021||JR||04-04-2001||08:19 PM||||Will:<P>No it wasn't cheap, around 10,000 dollars, probably not much more than a proper SB350 install.  But, the power is about the same as a fuel injected LT1, but the sound is unbeleivable, especially at 8000rpm---noone can match it and the looks I get from corvette and transam owners!!  They want to crawl and hide when they get there asses kicked!  Mine has a getrag 5speed, equal length headers with 2 3/4 pipe thru a cat and dual resonator tips.  Car has been lowered with poly alround.  Cars been lightened-no trunck, had NOX but coundn't keep a clutch in her very long.  Has MSD 6AL with spark control box.  Been running her for 2years no problem cept for clutch, centrforce in her with lightened flywheel.<P>Been watching this forum for years and finally joined so I could speak out about and support this underated mobile. It's nice that many are in to V8 installs, and I especially admire the Morthstar conversion.  Other than the Northstar conversion though, any V8 install maintining the stock transmissions is overkill as none of the boxes have limited slip.  With my built up 2.8 now yes a 3.2 requires no cutting, adaptor kits, bigger rads and the like, and my car is at least 200-300lbs lighter.<P>As far as dyno'ng, not necessary as GM virtually guarantees this Power, just follow the manual:Chevrolet Power-The Official Factory Performance Guide.<P>Thankyou Pennock for this fabulous forum, most interesting info.<P>My next project is a nodufued 3.4TDC I have sitting is storage.  Look out because that engine has the most potential of all the V6's--4-valve breathing. Oh boy,  saving my money!!<BR><P>------------------<BR>85GT (daily driver 120,000 miles)<BR>88GT (highly modified sunday driver)||||reg||1
Z||000022||DoD||04-04-2001||10:20 PM||||THANK YOU, JR!<P>I have been reading what people are saying about the 2.8 V6 in Fieros, and many people are not giving it enough respect.<P>I agree with everything you've said, and I did a similar buildup with my 2.8 for similar reasons.  (No titanium on mine, though...yet)<P>You are right about blowing away the V8 powered cars, too.  I have been saying this for a long time, to other people in other places, and no one would believe me.  In fact, I don't even think the people I have blown away believe it, either.<P>I limit my engine to short jaunts to 7000rpm, because we are still using the V6 rods, but just about everything inside that engine is precision machined and/or custom-built.<P>My point there, is:<P>To do the engine right, you will leave the capabilities of the stock computer WAY behind.  Also, though, I think it might not be completely accurate to think in terms of how much horsepower the computer can handle before it craps out.  There are just certain parameters that must remain within certain ranges for the program to function properly, regardless of horsepower output.<P>For instance, if you put the wrong cam in there, and your vacuum drops abysmally low, the computer will not be able to "handle it", but your engine will actually have LESS horsepower than stock.<P>My experience with my Fiero and its continual modifications are a lot of fun.  I enjoy my car thoroughly.||||reg||1
Z||000023||Will||04-04-2001||11:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JR:<BR><B>Will:<BR>My next project is a nodufued 3.4TDC I have sitting is storage.  Look out because that engine has the most potential of all the V6's--4-valve breathing. Oh boy,  saving my money!!<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>How do you plan on doing that? Custom billet cams?<BR>I'd heard that the timing belt couldn't handle Olds motorsports quad 4 springs. Are you going to convert it to chain drive?<P>I've been thinking for a while that that engine should be able to pull off 350+ HP at 8500 RPM.<BR>Billet small block rods with a forged turbo Grand Prix crankshaft...||||reg||1
Z||000024||terryk||04-05-2001||12:19 AM||||"To do the engine right, you will leave the capabilities of the stock computer WAY behind."<P>Why's that?||||reg||5
Z||000025||DoD||04-05-2001||05:45 PM||||To be a little more specific, it is not the capability of the ECM unit we are leaving behind (the hardware), but the capabilities of the program it runs and the parameters that are stored there (the software).<P>One of the main methods for making more power with an engine is to extend its operating range (more RPM) and burn more fuel (by flowing more air and richening the mixture).<P>This is accomplished, mainly, by changing the camshaft and fuel injectors significantly, and this will entail re-programming the "chip" that goes in the computer.<P>You don't have to go very far before you find that your fuel demands are more than your chip is "allowed" to deliver, and if you proceed without changing the program, you will burn pistons (if it runs at all).<P>You would also be surprised at the limitations of off-the-shelf chips like the HyperTech chips.  My 2.8 V6 is not as "radical" as I'd like, but we still had to have a custom chip programmed for it, because there was nothing that could handle the demands of the engine, the way it was built.<P>Now that we've gone this far with it, though, I plan on going back into it before too much longer...maybe next year or something like that.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000026||Cooter||04-05-2001||05:50 PM||||I wonder if anyone here could make a custom chip to run a preformance engine correctly?||||reg||1
Z||000027||DoD||04-05-2001||06:28 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.fastchip.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fastchip.com</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.superchips.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.superchips.com</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000028||terryk||04-05-2001||07:10 PM||||Cooter - ?||||reg||1
Z||000029||88formula||04-05-2001||08:16 PM|||| I hope that some members realize that the tuned port injection used on the fiero was not designed for high rpm engines. The long narrow intake runners are designed to promote low rpm torque and not high rpm horsepower. You have to keep that in mind when you select a camshaft as everything including the computer program is meant to work as a system.  <P>I think it is very possible to get 300 horsepower with the 2.8-liter engine if done properly.<P>Oliver, I don't think you can attain 220 horsepower from your 2.8-liter engine with the fiero's intake and exhaust systems. You would have to ditch the tuned port injection and use headers to get 220 horsepower naturally aspired. You would also have to do something with the heads. <BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000030||Turbo1dr||04-05-2001||08:29 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by olliver:<BR><B>I am just about to under go a HUGE engine rebuild (2.8L).  The guy who's doing it wants to know what the max h.p. that the computer can handle?...will it go into shut down?...<BR>If anyone has ANY info this would be great.<P>I was guaranteed 220 h.p. but i don't want the computer to crash.<P>Thanx <BR>Olliver</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>It believe your goals can be achived with the stock ECM. No one ever thought a Buick Grand National would EVER be as fast they are today. My Malibu (Grand National engine) still uses the stock ECM and has very little trouble controlling mixture. In fact it has so much drivability my mother can go get her grocery's in it!!!  I'm running mid 10's in the 1/4 and all I can say is... it's almost all in the chip programming!!! Of course since I am using a turbocharger on mine the cam doesn't have to be radical so drivability is excellent. <P>------------------<BR>86 SE Fiero converted to GT (Buick Turbo V6 transplant in progress)<P><B>78 Malibu</B> 3.8L Turbo Buick<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Homex.html" TARGET=_blank>TURBO WONDER</A><BR>Best 8th: 1.548, 6.62@106.32  3450lb<BR>TE-70 3 bolt, Ported Stock Iron Heads and Intake<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Modifications_files/Intercooler.jpg" TARGET=_blank>2.5 stock intercoolers</A><BR>Vernon Swindell, Jr.||||reg||1
Z||000031||DoD||04-05-2001||09:03 PM||||You have an excellent point about using forced induction vs. normal aspiration to gain the horsepower, especially in a computer-controlled car.  The compression ratio can be kept low, and the camshaft will end up comparatively mild, too.  But it seems to me that this would require a completely custom exhaust setup (especially for a turbocharger).<P>Of course, making big numbers still requires a strong bottom end, no matter where the power is coming from.<P>By the way, I like that turbo Buick V6.  I remember when they came out with that engine for the Regal T-Type and Grand Nationals and GNXs, and I went to the dealer and got some sales promotional materials on it (and I still have that stuff, buried somewhere).  At the time, it was probably the most "high-tech" thing on the road.  One of the best engines ever designed, in my opinion.<P>Anyway, keep up the good work.  Are you going to have to modify the Fiero very much to get the Buick V6 to fit?  That is one of the main reasons I stuck with my 2.8, was to keep from having to rebuild the whole car just to put a different engine in it for more power.<P>Of course, I went with the naturally aspirated approach, so I ended up with an engine that has a "personality" in traffic.<P>A personality that I enjoy, by the way.||||reg||1
Z||000032||DoD||04-05-2001||09:13 PM||||Uhh, dude...I just checked out your website about that Buick turbo V6 powered Malibu.<P>That is a SERIOUS automobile.  The amount of boost you are running is almost at the point where your engine will be picking up power from the turbos pushing the pistons down in the cylinders (if you're not there already)!<P>[whew!]<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000033||Cooter||04-05-2001||09:54 PM||||Terry-    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000034||Turbo1dr||04-05-2001||10:03 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DoD:<BR>[B<P>The amount of boost you are running is almost at the point where your engine will be picking up power from the turbos pushing the pistons down in the cylinders (if you're not there already)!<P>[whew!][/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yep you're right! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  The heads have reciever grooves so the gaskets won't push out and the crank is held in place by a girdle to help keep from driving over it. 30psi is on race gas. 17psi is about max for pump gas.<BR><P>------------------<BR>86 SE Fiero converted to GT (Buick Turbo V6 transplant in progress)<P><B>78 Malibu</B> 3.8L Turbo Buick<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Homex.html" TARGET=_blank>TURBO WONDER</A><BR>Best 8th: 1.548, 6.62@106.32  3450lb<BR>TE-70 3 bolt, Ported Stock Iron Heads and Intake<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Modifications_files/Intercooler.jpg" TARGET=_blank>2.5 stock intercoolers</A><BR>Vernon Swindell, Jr.||||reg||1
